  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-002",
   "finding": "report #17, #18, #34",
   "old": "- **OPA as the primary long-range automotive lidar** (range ≥200 m on a 10%-reflectance target, horizontal field ≥100°, resolution ≤0.1°): **unlikely this decade**. The obstacles are physical scaling laws, not only immaturity:\n  - degrees of freedom: the number of directions a steerer can address efficiently is roughly its number of independent controls (§C10.1);\n  - the second steering axis;",
   "new": "- **OPA as the primary long-range automotive lidar** [R] (range ≥200 m on a 10%-reflectance target, horizontal field ≥100°, resolution ≤0.1°): **unlikely this decade**. The obstacles are physical scaling laws, not only immaturity:\n  - degrees of freedom: for a steerer built from element-level controls, the number of directions it can address efficiently is roughly its number of independent controls, while whole-aperture phase ramps (mirror tilt, wavelength sweep) escape this at a cost in dwell, bandwidth or loss (§C10.1);\n  - the second steering axis: phase control on both axes needs about $10^6$ shifters at 0.05° over 120° × 25°, and wavelength steering over 25° needs ≈160 nm of tuning on Si (§C10.2, item 2);",
   "why": "Adds the deployability tag, limits the degrees-of-freedom rule to element-level steerers as §C10.1 does, and gives the reason behind the second-axis obstacle."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-003",
   "finding": "report #34",
   "old": "- **FPAs are the more natural solid-state coherent architecture.** A lens supplies a large physical aperture without per-element phase control.",
   "new": "- **FPAs are the more natural solid-state coherent architecture** [NT, mid-range; §C7a]. A lens supplies a large physical aperture without per-element phase control.",
   "why": "Adds the deployability tag that §C7a gives the Pointcloud FPA."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-004",
   "finding": "report #18; consistency flag (Pointcloud wavelength)",
   "old": "Full nominal coverage needs $A_{\\rm mode}\\lesssim\\tfrac{t^2}{2}N_{\\rm cell}\\lambda^2/\\Omega_{\\rm FOV}$, where $N_{\\rm cell}$ is the number of FPA cells, $\\lambda$ the wavelength, $\\Omega_{\\rm FOV}$ the solid angle of the field of view, $t$ the Gaussian aperture-truncation (power) factor of §C10.3b and ½ the single-polarization factor. For Pointcloud's coherent FPA (*Nature* 2026) that is under 1 mm equivalent diameter at full fill (beam footprints covering the whole field), and ≈6 mm at its ≈2% fill (§C10.3a).",
   "new": "For Pointcloud's coherent FPA (*Nature* 2026, 1.31 µm), covering its whole field would leave an $A_{\\rm mode}$ equal to that of a lens under 1 mm across; at the ≈2% nominal footprint fraction it actually uses (defined below), it gets ≈6 mm (§C10.3a, item 5).",
   "why": "Removes the five-symbol inequality from the summary (it is derived in §C10.3a, item 5), states the wavelength and uses the defined term instead of 'fill'."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-005",
   "finding": "report #24, #34; consistency flag (Lumotive range)",
   "old": "- **LC metasurfaces (Lumotive) are the only non-mechanical beam steerer found in commercial production.** A large, free-space-fed aperture addresses the solid-state *receive* problem. The limits are LC switching speed, one-dimensional steering, cold-temperature response and grating dispersion (the steering angle shifts with wavelength). Products so far reach ≈10–50 m (§C10.5).",
   "new": "- **LC metasurfaces (Lumotive) are the only non-mechanical beam steerer found in commercial production** [D]. A large, free-space-fed aperture could address the solid-state *receive* problem, collecting many spatial modes from the steered direction (§C10.2, item 6), although no shipping product documents a receive-path metasurface yet (§C10.5). The limits are LC switching speed, one-dimensional steering, cold-temperature response and grating dispersion (the steering angle shifts with wavelength). Shipping products and reference designs reach ≈10–50 m, and one module shown at CES 2026 claims up to 100 m (§C10.5).",
   "why": "Defines the receive problem where it is first used, matches §C10.5 (no receive-path product; Stella-2 at up to 100 m) and adds the deployability tag."
  },
  {
