  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-011",
   "finding": "report #22",
   "old": "  - Peak intensity scales as $N^2$, because the fields of $N$ elements add in phase in the main-lobe direction, while the angle-averaged intensity scales as $N$ and spreads over $N_{\\rm spot}$ spots. The main-lobe power fraction is therefore about $N/N_{\\rm spot}$, and the mean sidelobe level about $1/N$ relative to the peak:",
   "new": "  - Normalize the intensity radiated by one element to 1. The peak intensity is then $N^2$, because the fields of $N$ elements add in phase in the main-lobe direction, while the angle-averaged intensity is $N$, because elsewhere their random phases add in power. Measured in units of the full steering range, the main lobe is $1/N_{\\rm spot}$ wide, so it carries power $N^2/N_{\\rm spot}$ out of a total of $N$, a main-lobe power fraction of $N/N_{\\rm spot}$. The mean sidelobe level is $N/N^2=1/N$ of the peak:",
   "why": "Shows the skipped step from intensities to the power fraction."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-012",
   "finding": "report #20",
   "old": "For $N=256$ and $N_{\\rm spot}\\approx1{,}720$ it gave 12%, −12.6 dB and −24 dB.",
   "new": "For $N=256$ and $N_{\\rm spot}\\approx1{,}720$ ($N/N_{\\rm spot}=15\\%$) it gave 12%, −12.6 dB and −24 dB.",
   "why": "Gives the N/N_spot prediction for the second case too (256/1,720 = 0.149)."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-013",
   "finding": "report #12, #29; symbol scheme",
   "old": "the aperture length $D$ that gives the required beamwidth $\\theta$ (full width at half maximum, FWHM, in radians), and the resulting element count $N$:\n\n$$d\\le\\frac{\\lambda}{2\\sin(\\mathrm{FOV}/2)},\\qquad D=\\frac{0.886\\,\\lambda}{\\theta},\\qquad N=\\frac{D}{d} \\tag{130}$$\n\nHere FOV is the full alias-free field of view along that axis, and 0.886 is the FWHM of the far-field pattern of a uniformly illuminated aperture in units of $\\lambda/D$.\n\n| Alias-free FOV (no grating lobe inside the FOV) | Max pitch $\\lambda/(2\\sin\\tfrac{\\rm FOV}{2})$ | 0.1°: aperture / elements | 0.05° | 0.025° |",
   "new": "the aperture length $D$ that gives the required beamwidth $\\theta_{\\rm B}$ (full width at half maximum, FWHM, in radians), and the resulting element count $N$:\n\n$$d\\le\\frac{\\lambda}{2\\sin(\\mathrm{FOV}/2)},\\qquad D=\\frac{0.886\\,\\lambda}{\\theta_{\\rm B}},\\qquad N=\\frac{D}{d} \\tag{130}$$\n\nHere FOV is the full alias-free field of view along that axis, and 0.886 is the FWHM of the far-field pattern of a uniformly illuminated aperture in units of $\\lambda/D$. The table evaluates Eq. (130) for three fields and three beamwidths $\\theta_{\\rm B}$.\n\n| Alias-free FOV (no grating lobe inside the FOV) | Max pitch $\\lambda/(2\\sin\\tfrac{\\rm FOV}{2})$ | Beamwidth $\\theta_{\\rm B}=0.1^\\circ$: aperture / elements | $\\theta_{\\rm B}=0.05^\\circ$ | $\\theta_{\\rm B}=0.025^\\circ$ |",
   "why": "Beamwidth gets its own symbol (theta is the emission angle a few lines later); the table gets a lead-in and headers that say the columns are beamwidths."
  },
