  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-015",
   "finding": "report #13, #33",
   "old": "- 2D phase control for 2,400×500 spots would need about 1.2 M phase shifters. Published 2D arrays are orders of magnitude smaller.\n- The standard answer is **wavelength steering** with grating emitters [E]. A grating emitter is a waveguide with a periodic corrugation that leaks light out at an angle $\\theta$ set by the wavelength $\\lambda$, so tuning the laser steers the beam along the second axis. Its steering rate $d\\theta/d\\lambda$ (radians of beam angle per nanometre of wavelength change) is",
   "new": "- 2D phase control for 2,400×500 spots (0.05° over 120° × 25°) would need about $2{,}400\\times500=1.2\\times10^{6}$ phase shifters [E]. Published 2D arrays are orders of magnitude smaller [U].\n- The standard answer is **wavelength steering** with grating emitters. A grating emitter is a waveguide with a periodic corrugation of period $\\Lambda$ that leaks light out at an angle $\\theta$ given by the grating equation $\\sin\\theta=n_{\\rm eff}-\\lambda/\\Lambda$, where $\\lambda$ is the wavelength and $n_{\\rm eff}$ the effective index of the guided mode (the refractive index the light \"sees\" in the waveguide). Tuning the laser therefore steers the beam along the second axis. Differentiating the grating equation with respect to $\\lambda$, and writing $n_g=n_{\\rm eff}-\\lambda\\,dn_{\\rm eff}/d\\lambda$, gives the magnitude of the steering rate $d\\theta/d\\lambda$ (radians of beam angle per nanometre of wavelength change):",
   "why": "Writes the grating equation and the derivation step behind Eq. (131); puts [E] on the estimate rather than on common practice and marks the unsourced 2D-array claim [U]."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-016",
   "finding": "report #13, #33",
   "old": "  where $n_g$ is the group index of the waveguide (the ratio of the speed of light in vacuum to the speed of a light pulse in the guide). Near normal emission this gives:",
   "new": "  Here $n_g$, the group index of the waveguide, is the ratio of the speed of light in vacuum to the speed of a light pulse in the guide. Near normal emission Eq. (131) gives [E]:",
   "why": "n_g is now introduced before the equation; the [E] tag moves to the Si and SiN estimates."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-017",
   "finding": "report #28",
   "old": "- The laser must therefore be **widely tunable, narrow-linewidth (≲20–55 kHz at 300 m for a 1–3 dB coherent loss, §C7b subsection 2(e); Waymo designs to ≲60–120 kHz at 250 m, §C10.3), linearly chirped and cheap** at once. Linewidth is the spectral width of the laser; it limits the coherence length and hence the range over which the return can still be mixed coherently with the LO.",
   "new": "- The laser must therefore be **widely tunable, linearly chirped, cheap and narrow in linewidth** at once. Linewidth is the spectral width of the laser; it limits the coherence length and hence the range over which the return can still be mixed coherently with the LO. A 1–3 dB coherent loss at 300 m allows ≲18–55 kHz (§C7b subsection 2(e)); Waymo's design value of ≲60–120 kHz at 250 m accepts a 2.7–5.5 dB loss (§C7a(f); §C10.3).",
   "why": "Un-nests the parentheses, uses the 18.3 kHz of the §C7b table and says why Waymo's limit is looser."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-018",
   "finding": "symbol scheme (chirp bandwidth B, as in §C0 and Eq. (136))",
   "old": "A chirp spanning a frequency range $\\Delta f$ changes the wavelength by $\\Delta\\lambda=\\lambda^2\\Delta f/c$, with $c$ the speed of light.",
   "new": "A chirp of bandwidth $B$ (the frequency range it sweeps) changes the wavelength by $\\Delta\\lambda=\\lambda^2B/c$, with $c$ the speed of light.",
   "why": "Delta f is reserved for the electrical bandwidth; the chirp bandwidth is B elsewhere in the report."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-019",
   "finding": "report #37",
   "old": "A residual random phase error of rms $\\sigma=0.3$ rad lowers that to **82%**, as the Strehl-type factor $e^{-\\sigma^2}=e^{-0.09}=0.914$ predicts ($0.90\\times0.914=0.82$). The Strehl factor is the fraction of peak intensity kept when uncorrelated phase errors of rms $\\sigma$ (in radians) are present.",
   "new": "The Strehl factor $e^{-\\sigma^2}$ is the fraction of peak intensity kept when uncorrelated phase errors of rms $\\sigma$ (in radians) are present. For a residual random phase error of $\\sigma=0.3$ rad it is $e^{-0.09}=0.914$, which lowers the main-lobe share to **82%** ($0.90\\times0.914=0.82$), as the Monte Carlo finds.",
   "why": "Defines the Strehl factor before using it."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-020",
   "finding": "report #13",
   "old": "The emission angle of a grating depends on the effective index $n_{\\rm eff}$ of the guided mode (the refractive index the light \"sees\" in the waveguide), and $n_{\\rm eff}$ changes with temperature at a rate $dn_{\\rm eff}/dT$ (the thermo-optic coefficient of the mode, per kelvin). Differentiating the grating equation at fixed wavelength gives the angle drift $\\Delta\\theta$ for a temperature change $\\Delta T$, and dividing it by the steering rate of Eq. (131) gives the wavelength change $\\Delta\\lambda_{\\rm comp}$ needed per kelvin to hold the angle:",
   "new": "The emission angle depends on the effective index $n_{\\rm eff}$ of the guided mode (item 2), and $n_{\\rm eff}$ changes with temperature at a rate $dn_{\\rm eff}/dT$ (the thermo-optic coefficient of the mode, per kelvin). At fixed wavelength the grating equation gives $\\cos\\theta\\,\\Delta\\theta=\\Delta n_{\\rm eff}$, hence the angle drift $\\Delta\\theta$ for a temperature change $\\Delta T$. Dividing it by the steering rate of Eq. (131) gives the wavelength change $\\Delta\\lambda_{\\rm comp}$ needed per kelvin to hold the angle:",
   "why": "Shows the differentiation step; n_eff is defined at its first use in item 2."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-021",
   "finding": "report #32",
   "old": "$\\Delta\\theta\\approx1.6\\times10^{-4}$ rad/K, about **0.009°/K**, i.e. about $0.0092\\times145\\ {\\rm K}\\approx$ **1.3° over −40 to +105 °C**, or about 25 cells at 0.05° ($1.3^\\circ/0.05^\\circ\\approx26$);",
   "new": "$\\Delta\\theta/\\Delta T\\approx1.6\\times10^{-4}$ rad/K, about **0.009°/K**, i.e. about $0.0092\\times145\\ {\\rm K}\\approx$ **1.3° over −40 to +105 °C**, or about 26 cells at 0.05° ($1.3^\\circ/0.05^\\circ=26$);",
