   "id": "09_C10_part1-023",
   "finding": "report #39; symbol scheme",
   "old": "The number of detected signal photons $N_s$ from one pulse is the number of transmitted photons, times the fraction of them that a Lambertian target scatters into the receive mode, times the detection efficiency, times the OPA's one-way transmission squared:\n\n  $$N_s=\\frac{E_{\\rm pulse}}{E_{\\rm ph}}\\,\\frac{\\rho A_{\\rm mode}}{\\pi R^2}\\,\\eta\\,\\eta_{\\rm OPA}^2 \\tag{134}$$\n\n  Here $E_{\\rm pulse}$ is the transmitted energy per shot,",
   "new": "The number of detected signal photons $N_s$ from one sample (one chirp or pulse) is the number of transmitted photons, times the fraction of them that a Lambertian target scatters into the receive mode, times the detection efficiency, times the OPA's one-way transmission squared:\n\n  $$N_s=\\frac{E_{\\rm pt}}{E_{\\rm ph}}\\,\\frac{\\rho A_{\\rm mode}}{\\pi R^2}\\,\\eta\\,\\eta_{\\rm OPA}^2 \\tag{134}$$\n\n  Here $E_{\\rm pt}$ is the optical energy transmitted toward one point,",
   "why": "The example is a 5 µs chirp, not a pulse; E_pt is the report-wide symbol for energy per point."
  },
