  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-026",
   "finding": "report #37",
   "old": "  - A non-fading return needs about 21 [E: for a constant signal the detection probability is the Marcum function $Q_1(\\sqrt{2\\bar n},\\sqrt{2\\gamma_{\\rm th}})$ with $\\gamma_{\\rm th}=\\ln10^{6}=13.8$, and $Q_1=0.9$ at $\\bar n=20.8$]. The Marcum Q-function $Q_1(a,b)$ is the probability that the envelope of a constant signal of normalized amplitude $a$ plus complex Gaussian noise exceeds a normalized threshold $b$.",
   "new": "  - A non-fading return needs about 21. For a constant signal the detection probability is the Marcum Q-function $Q_1(a,b)$: the probability that the envelope of a constant signal of normalized amplitude $a$ plus complex Gaussian noise exceeds a normalized threshold $b$. With $a=\\sqrt{2\\bar n}$, $b=\\sqrt{2\\gamma_{\\rm th}}$ and $\\gamma_{\\rm th}=\\ln10^{6}=13.8$, $Q_1=0.9$ at $\\bar n=20.8$ [E].",
   "why": "Defines the Marcum function before using it."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-027",
   "finding": "report #38; consistency flag (120 photoelectrons)",
   "old": "The report's per-point rule (§C0 subsection 10.3: at most $10^{-3}$ false points per point over a 0–300 m search, with the echo's peak found in both chirp ramps) raises them to ≈259 for one look, 120 for two, ≈88 for four and ≈82 for eight looks, and ≈39 for a non-fading return.",
   "new": "The report's per-point rule is stricter: at most $10^{-3}$ false points per point over a 0–300 m search, with the echo's peak found in both chirp ramps (§C0 subsection 10.3). It raises the requirements to ≈259 detected photons for one look, 120 for two, 88.5 for four, 82 for eight and 39 for a non-fading return. The 120 for two looks is not the 120 of the §C7a model, which is the four-look requirement of 88.5 with a 1.36× margin.",
   "why": "Splits a 55-word sentence, uses the 88.5 that the power figures use, and separates two different 120s."
  },
