   "id": "09_C10_part1-031",
   "finding": "report #14",
   "old": "- Each channel needs its own receiver and digitizer: 0.2–1 GHz beat at 300 m, i.e. 0.4–2 GS/s (gigasamples per second) real sampling, doubled for up/down chirps, plus about ±0.1 GHz of Doppler.",
   "new": "- Each channel needs its own receiver and digitizer. For a single ramp, the beat at 300 m is 0.2 GHz at 10 µs and 1 GHz at 2 µs, which needs 0.4–2 GS/s (gigasamples per second) of real sampling, as derived next.",
   "why": "States the single-ramp case first; what doubles is explained after Eq. (136)."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-032",
   "finding": "report #14",
   "old": "Real sampling needs at least twice the beat frequency (the Nyquist criterion), hence 0.4–2 GS/s. From the Doppler term of Eq. (136), ±0.1 GHz covers radial speeds up to",
   "new": "Real sampling needs at least twice the beat frequency (the Nyquist criterion), hence 0.4–2 GS/s. If the dwell is split into an up-ramp and a down-ramp, each ramp is half as long, so by Eq. (136) the beat frequency and the sampling rate double, to 0.4–2 GHz and 0.8–4 GS/s. Doppler adds about ±0.1 GHz: from the Doppler term of Eq. (136), ±0.1 GHz covers radial speeds up to",
   "why": "Says what doubles for up/down chirps and why."
  },
