   "finding": "follow-up #6",
   "old": "| Analog Photonics, 9,216 (VLSI 2023) [I/S] | 9,216 | About 50° × 11° | 0.05° |",
   "new": "| Analog Photonics, 9,216 (VLSI 2023) [I/S] | 9,216 | About 50° × 11° | 0.05°, axis not stated [U] |",
   "why": "The 0.05° cannot be the phase-axis beamwidth of 9,216 elements (derivation added below the table)."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-035",
   "finding": "follow-up #6",
   "old": "- ZJU driver power: about 1 mW per element times 4,096 elements is 4.1 W.",
   "new": "- ZJU driver power: about 1 mW per element times 4,096 elements is 4.1 W.\n- Analog Photonics 9,216: by Eq. (130) a 50° alias-free field allows a pitch of at most $1.55\\ \\mu{\\rm m}/(2\\sin25^\\circ)=1.83$ µm. All 9,216 elements on one phase axis would then span 7.1–16.9 mm (pitch $\\lambda/2=0.775$ µm to 1.83 µm), with a FWHM of $0.886\\lambda/D=0.011^\\circ$ to $0.886\\times1.55\\ \\mu{\\rm m}/16.9\\ {\\rm mm}=8.1\\times10^{-5}$ rad $=0.005^\\circ$. The listed 0.05° is about 5–11 times wider, so it cannot be the phase-axis beamwidth of a single 9,216-element row; which axis it describes is not stated.",
   "why": "Shows why the 9,216-element beam figure is marked [U]."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-036",
   "finding": "follow-up #12; consistency flag (resolvable spots)",
   "old": "1. **The phase axis is no longer the barrier.** Element counts of 10³–10⁴ at λ/2–1 µm pitch exist, with about 10⁴ resolvable spots in one axis (8,192 elements over 100°: $100^\\circ/0.01^\\circ=10^{4}$ beamwidths). Multi-reticle stitching has been demonstrated (49,152 elements).",
   "new": "1. **The phase axis is no longer the barrier.** Element counts of 10³–10⁴ at λ/2–1 µm pitch exist. The 8,192-element array resolves about 8,100 spots over its 100° phase axis: its 8.19 mm aperture spans $8.19\\ {\\rm mm}\\times2\\sin50^\\circ/1.55\\ \\mu{\\rm m}\\approx8{,}100$ beamwidths in sine space, close to its element count, as the rule of §C10.1 predicts (dividing by the broadside beamwidth instead, $100^\\circ/0.01^\\circ$, gives $10^{4}$). Multi-reticle stitching of more than 49,152 elements (24 OPAs combined coherently over four reticles) has been reported in a conference abstract [I], without ranging.",
   "why": "Counts spots in sine space as §C10.1 does, and matches the table (conference abstract, >49,152, no ranging)."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-037",
   "finding": "report #11",
   "old": "DTU's 120-element array spread over 6 mm has a *simulated* main-lobe fraction of 0.8% and a *measured* floor only 3–6 dB below the peak.",
   "new": "DTU's 120-element array spread over 6 mm has a *simulated* main-lobe fraction of 0.8%. Its *measured* floor, only 3–6 dB below the peak, was recorded with a camera of 0.13° resolution, whose pixels each integrate many sidelobe spots of width $\\lambda/D$, with $D=6$ mm the aperture: $1.55\\ \\mu{\\rm m}/6\\ {\\rm mm}=2.6\\times10^{-4}$ rad $=0.015^\\circ$, about $0.13/0.015\\approx9$ spots per pixel along that axis. A pixel's floor therefore sits far above the per-spot mean of §C10.1, $1/N=1/120$, i.e. −21 dB [E].",
   "why": "Explains why the measured floor is far above the 1/N rule (the N/N_spot part needs the array geometry; see skipped)."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-038",
   "finding": "report #32",
   "old": "its peak is about 9 dB higher.",
   "new": "its peak is about 8 dB higher.",
   "why": "Matches the 8.4 dB computed in the same bullet."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-039",
   "finding": "report #27",
   "old": "   - Correlated waveguide-width errors make uncalibrated phases effectively random: about 12 rad per nm of width error per mm of Si path, and the Si phase-coherence length is about 1.6–4 mm [I].",
   "new": "   - Waveguide-width errors, correlated along each guide, accumulate phase at about 12 rad per nm of width error per mm of Si path, so after a few millimetres the uncalibrated phases are effectively random. The Si phase-coherence length, the guide length beyond which two nominally identical paths no longer keep a predictable phase relation, is about 1.6–4 mm [I].",
   "why": "Removes the apparent 'correlated ... random' contradiction and defines the phase-coherence length."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-040",
   "finding": "consistency flag (FSOC-B1 loss)",
   "old": "The npj review's itemized state of the art sums to about 4–10 dB. Every dB counts twice (§C10.2, item 5).",
   "new": "The npj review's itemized state of the art sums to about 4–10 dB, and Analog Photonics specifies its FSOC-B1 steerer at ≤7.5 dB from fibre to beam (products table below; vendor claim [U]). Every dB counts twice (§C10.2, item 5).",
   "why": "Mentions the vendor loss figure given later in the same subsection."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-041",
   "finding": "report #9",
   "old": "   - Energy per point for OPA lidars is 1,360–1,700 nJ, against 46 nJ for the Pointcloud FPA and 212 nJ for Rogers et al. (Pointcloud's comparison table) [S].",
   "new": "   - Optical energy per point for OPA lidars is 1,360–1,700 nJ, the upper value being the 8,192-element array's at 35 m on a 10% target. Pointcloud's FPA uses 46 nJ (at 65 m and 30%), and the 512-pixel coherent receiver array of Rogers et al. (*Nature* 2021, §C10.4) 212 nJ. All four values come from Pointcloud's comparison table [S].",
   "why": "Says the energies are optical, ties 1,700 nJ to its device and range, and identifies Rogers et al."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-042",
   "finding": "report #9",
   "old": "   - At 12 Mpts/s this is about 530–670 W ($44\\ \\mu{\\rm J}\\times12\\times10^{6}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}=530$ W, $56\\ \\mu{\\rm J}\\times12\\times10^{6}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}=670$ W): a gap of one to two orders of magnitude, not a calibration detail.",
   "new": "   - At 12 Mpts/s this is about 530–670 W of optical power ($44\\ \\mu{\\rm J}\\times12\\times10^{6}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}=530$ W, $56\\ \\mu{\\rm J}\\times12\\times10^{6}\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}=670$ W). That is 15–19 times the 35 W that the whole FS-4S module draws and 66–84 times the 8 W of the Hesai ATX (table below): a gap of one to two orders of magnitude, not a calibration detail.",
   "why": "States what the gap is measured against (530/35 = 15, 670/35 = 19, 530/8 = 66, 670/8 = 84)."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-043",
   "finding": "report #34",
   "old": "[P, company datasheets Rev A, June–July 2025, rendered and read; figures are vendor claims]",
   "new": "[P, company datasheets Rev A, June–July 2025; figures are vendor claims]",
   "why": "Removes a working note."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-044",
   "finding": "report #10; follow-up #9",
   "old": "**Who they are.** An MIT-linked Boston company founded by Michael Watts [I]. Third-party databases give 2012 as the founding year; the company's own timeline begins with a 2016 award under DARPA's MOABB (Modular Optical Aperture Building Blocks) program. Its platform is MOABB-funded 300 mm silicon photonics with flip-chip CMOS drivers (electronic driver chips bonded face-down onto the photonic chip). Company milestones: a Tier-1 (a supplier selling directly to carmakers) development agreement (2018), on-car tests (2019), a second Tier-1 agreement (2020) and a narrow-linewidth FMCW laser (2021).",
   "new": "**Who they are.** Analog Photonics (Boston) was founded about 2012 by Michael Watts of MIT, who remains CEO [P/S]. Federal records show subawards from Nov 2013 and SBIR (US Small Business Innovation Research) awards from 2015 [I]; the company's own timeline starts with a 2016 award under the MOABB (Modular Optical Aperture Building Blocks) program of DARPA, the US Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency (§C3a). Its platform is MOABB-funded 300 mm silicon photonics with flip-chip CMOS drivers (electronic driver chips bonded face-down onto the photonic chip). Company milestones [P]: a joint development agreement with a Tier 1 (a supplier selling directly to carmakers) in 2018, on-car tests in 2019, a second Tier 1 agreement in 2020 and a narrow-linewidth FMCW laser in 2021. Funding, size and the departure of the lead author of its OPA papers are covered in §C3a.",
   "why": "Aligns the profile with §C3a (founding, tags, earlier federal money) and points there instead of duplicating it."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-045",
   "finding": "report #10, #34; follow-up #2",
   "old": "Its 2023 company-authored IEEE Spectrum article promised \"large numbers of samples\" for automotive in 2025. **No production award or sample shipment was found.** Funding is mostly government contracts and grants; amounts are not disclosed, so treat any figure with a grain of salt. A 2025 talk by the CEO emphasized drone and counter-drone (counter-UAS) lidar and drone-to-drone optical links [I].",
   "new": "Its 2023 company-authored IEEE Spectrum article promised \"large numbers of samples\" for automotive in 2025 [P]. **No production award or sample shipment was found.** Funding is mostly US government money: \\$45.2 M of federal prime obligations (money committed under contracts and grants the company holds itself) and \\$34.2 M of subawards (money passed on by other prime holders) [I], ≈\\$79 M over 2013–2026 [E] (§C3a); no venture round was found. A 2025 talk by the CEO emphasized drone and counter-drone (counter-UAS, where UAS means unmanned aircraft system) lidar and drone-to-drone optical links [P].",
   "why": "'Not disclosed' contradicted §C3a's USAspending figures; company statements carry [P]."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-046",
   "finding": "follow-up #5; follow-up section 4 flag ('AP')",
   "old": "It is far beyond the 14–17° in AP's papers [U] |",
   "new": "It is far beyond the 15–17° wavelength axes of Analog Photonics' 512- and 8,192-element papers [U] |",
   "why": "Matches the device table (15° and 17°) and spells out the undefined 'AP'."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-047",
   "finding": "follow-up #4, #8",
   "old": "| **FSOC-B1** / OPA-B1 steerer | 50° × 14°, 1 mrad beam, ≤7.5 dB fibre-to-beam loss at up to 400 mW in, <10 µs random access, <5 W | The same engine sold for free-space optical links |",
   "new": "| **FSOC-B1** / OPA-B1 steerer | 50° × 14°, 1 mrad beam, ≤7.5 dB fibre-to-beam loss at up to 400 mW in, <10 µs random access, <5 W [U] | The same OPA engine sold for free-space optical links. If its 14° axis is wavelength-steered, <10 µs access needs a laser that retunes about 30× faster than RAY-H1 (<300 µs) |",
   "why": "Flags the untraced datasheet and the conflict with RAY-H1's retune time (300/10 = 30)."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-048",
   "finding": "follow-up #5",
   "old": "against $n_g\\approx4.2$ for a Si strip waveguide ($32/4.2\\approx8$).",
   "new": "against $n_g\\approx4.2$ for a Si strip waveguide ($32/4.2\\approx8$). Over the whole ≈95 nm C+L band it would still need $70^\\circ/95\\ {\\rm nm}=0.74^\\circ/{\\rm nm}=0.0129$ rad/nm, a factor $(n_g-\\sin\\theta)/\\cos\\theta=0.0129\\times1550\\approx20$, about $20/4.2\\approx4.8$ times a Si strip; either is far beyond the published arrays.\n- FS-4S: its 14° wavelength-steered axis over the ≈95 nm C+L band needs $14^\\circ/95\\ {\\rm nm}=0.147^\\circ/{\\rm nm}$, consistent with the 8,192-element array's 0.14°/nm. Over RAY-H1's 60 nm it would need $14^\\circ/60\\ {\\rm nm}=0.23^\\circ/{\\rm nm}=0.00407$ rad/nm, a factor $0.00407\\times1550\\approx6.3$, 1.5 times a Si strip ($6.3/4.2$).",
   "why": "Checks both modules against both laser bands, since the datasheets do not say which laser each uses."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-049",
   "finding": "report #34",
   "old": "Per 2024 press reports, its target price is under \\$200 [I].",
   "new": "Per 2024 press reports, its target price is under \\$200 [T].",
   "why": "Press reports are [T] in the legend."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-050",
   "finding": "report #36",
   "old": "at 230 m at 10%, 1.5 MHz, 0.1° × 0.05° and ≤5 W",
   "new": "at 230 m at 10%, 1.5 MHz (1.5 Mpts/s if each pulse yields one point), 0.1° × 0.05° and ≤5 W",
   "why": "Relates the quoted rate to the point-rate unit used elsewhere."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-051",
   "finding": "follow-up residual (report #1)",
   "old": "- *Speckle.* The coherent thresholds $N_{\\rm req}=10$–30 are roughly those for a non-fading return.",
   "new": "- *Speckle.* The coherent thresholds $N_{\\rm req}=10$–30 bracket the single-bin non-fading requirement (≈21 detected photons) and lie below the per-point one (≈39).",
   "why": "States how the thresholds compare with the two non-fading requirements of §C10.2, item 5."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-052",
   "finding": "symbol scheme (M reserved for APD gain); acronym at first use",
   "old": "- *Ideal modes.* Beams are ideal TEM00 Gaussians ($M^2=1$) with perfect mode matching and alignment, and there is no atmospheric turbulence.",
   "new": "- *Ideal modes.* Beams are ideal TEM00 Gaussians (the fundamental transverse mode, with beam-quality factor $M^2=1$; this $M$ is not an APD gain) with perfect mode matching and alignment, and there is no atmospheric turbulence.",
   "why": "Defines TEM00 and M^2 at their first use in the section."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-053",
   "finding": "report #30, #38; symbol scheme",
   "old": "**One photon budget, two receiving rules** [E]. Both receivers share one transmitted energy per sample, but they differ in how much of the return they can use. Let $\\Omega_{\\rm FOV}$ be the solid angle of the field of view and $\\Omega_{\\rm cell}$ the solid angle of one angular sample (resolution cell), both in steradians. Let $N_{\\rm samp}\\simeq\\Omega_{\\rm FOV}/\\Omega_{\\rm cell}$ be the number of uniformly spaced samples per frame, $E_s=P/(f_{\\rm frame}N_{\\rm samp})$ their transmitted energy (average optical power $P$, frame rate $f_{\\rm frame}$), and $E_{\\rm ph}=hc/\\lambda$ the photon energy at wavelength $\\lambda$. For a large Lambertian target that fills the illuminated patch, with end-to-end detection efficiency $\\eta$ (the fraction of photons arriving at the aperture that are detected, including optics transmission and quantum efficiency), reflectivity $\\rho$, range $R$ and receive-aperture area $A_{\\rm rx}$, the photons reaching a direct detector are approximately\n\n$$n_{\\rm direct}=\\frac{\\eta\\rho E_s}{E_{\\rm ph}}\\frac{A_{\\rm rx}}{\\pi R^2}\\ \\propto\\ A_{\\rm rx}\\Omega_{\\rm cell}. \\tag{137}$$",
   "new": "**One photon budget, two receiving rules** [E]. Both receivers share one transmitted energy per sample, but they differ in how much of the return they can use. The symbols are:\n- $\\Omega_{\\rm FOV}$, the solid angle of the field of view, and $\\Omega_{\\rm cell}$, the solid angle of one angular sample (resolution cell), both in steradians;\n- $N_{\\rm samp}\\simeq\\Omega_{\\rm FOV}/\\Omega_{\\rm cell}$, the number of uniformly spaced samples per frame;\n- $E_{\\rm pt}=P/(f_{\\rm frame}N_{\\rm samp})$, the optical energy transmitted toward one sample, with $P$ the average optical power and $f_{\\rm frame}$ the frame rate;\n- $E_{\\rm ph}=hc/\\lambda$, the photon energy at wavelength $\\lambda$;\n- $\\eta$, the end-to-end detection efficiency: the fraction of photons arriving at the aperture that are detected, including optics transmission and quantum efficiency;\n- $\\rho$, the target's reflectance; $R$, the range; and $A_{\\rm rx}$, the receive-aperture area.\n\nFor a large Lambertian target that fills the illuminated patch, the number of photons a direct-detection receiver detects is approximately\n\n$$n_{\\rm direct}=\\frac{\\eta\\rho E_{\\rm pt}}{E_{\\rm ph}}\\frac{A_{\\rm rx}}{\\pi R^2}\\ \\propto\\ A_{\\rm rx}\\Omega_{\\rm cell}. \\tag{137}$$",
   "why": "Turns the packed symbol sentence into a list, uses E_pt, and says that Eq. (137) counts detected photons (it includes eta)."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-054",
   "finding": "report #30; symbol scheme",
   "old": "($E_s\\propto1/N_{\\rm samp}\\propto\\Omega_{\\rm cell}$). Do not multiply by cell étendue again.",
   "new": "($E_{\\rm pt}\\propto1/N_{\\rm samp}\\propto\\Omega_{\\rm cell}$). The receiver's étendue adds no second factor: the lens collects light from the whole illuminated cell, whatever its size.",
   "why": "Replaces a note-to-self with the reason."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-055",
   "finding": "report #39; symbol scheme",
   "old": "- **A matched single-mode coherent receiver uses a *mode-equivalent collecting area* instead of the hard area.** The mode-equivalent collecting area $A_{\\rm mode}$ is the effective area with which a single-mode receiver collects diffusely scattered light: only light matching its one spatial mode counts, so its area and accepted solid angle together are limited to about $\\lambda^2$. The coherent count is Eq. (137) with $A_{\\rm mode}$ in place of $A_{\\rm rx}$:\n\n  $$n_{\\rm coh}=\\frac{\\eta\\rho E_s}{E_{\\rm ph}}\\frac{A_{\\rm mode}}{\\pi R^2},\\qquad A_{\\rm mode}\\sim\\frac{\\lambda^2}{\\Omega_{\\rm cell}} \\tag{138}$$\n\n  If the received mode is widened to fill every angular cell, its best-order scaling is $A_{\\rm mode}\\sim\\lambda^2/\\Omega_{\\rm cell}$, and the two cell-angle factors (in $E_s$ and in $A_{\\rm mode}$) cancel.",
   "new": "- **A matched single-mode coherent receiver uses the mode-equivalent collecting area $A_{\\rm mode}$ of §C10.2, item 5, instead of the hard area.** Only light matching its one spatial mode counts, so its area and accepted solid angle together are limited to about $\\lambda^2$. The coherent count is Eq. (137) with $A_{\\rm mode}$ in place of $A_{\\rm rx}$, which is also Eq. (134) with $\\eta_{\\rm OPA}=1$:\n\n  $$n_{\\rm coh}=\\frac{\\eta\\rho E_{\\rm pt}}{E_{\\rm ph}}\\frac{A_{\\rm mode}}{\\pi R^2},\\qquad A_{\\rm mode}\\sim\\frac{\\lambda^2}{\\Omega_{\\rm cell}} \\tag{138}$$\n\n  If the received mode is widened to fill every angular cell, its best-order scaling is $A_{\\rm mode}\\sim\\lambda^2/\\Omega_{\\rm cell}$, and the two cell-angle factors (in $E_{\\rm pt}$ and in $A_{\\rm mode}$) cancel.",
   "why": "Points to the earlier definition of A_mode instead of repeating it, and links Eq. (138) to Eq. (134)."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-056",
   "finding": "symbol scheme",
   "old": "solving for $P=f_{\\rm frame}N_{\\rm samp}E_s$ gives",
   "new": "solving for $P=f_{\\rm frame}N_{\\rm samp}E_{\\rm pt}$ gives",
   "why": "E_pt for the energy per point."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-057",
   "finding": "report #30",
   "old": "$$P_{\\rm coh}=\\frac{N_{\\rm req}E_{\\rm ph}\\pi R^2f_{\\rm frame}\\,\\Omega_{\\rm FOV}}{\\rho\\,\\lambda^2\\,\\eta},\\qquad P_{\\rm ToF}=\\frac{N_{\\rm req}E_{\\rm ph}\\pi R^2f_{\\rm frame}\\,\\Omega_{\\rm FOV}}{\\rho\\,A_{\\rm rx}\\Omega_{\\rm cell}\\,\\eta} \\tag{139}$$",
   "new": "$$\\begin{aligned}P_{\\rm coh}&=\\frac{N_{\\rm req}E_{\\rm ph}\\pi R^2f_{\\rm frame}\\,\\Omega_{\\rm FOV}}{\\rho\\,\\lambda^2\\,\\eta},\\\\ P_{\\rm ToF}&=\\frac{N_{\\rm req}E_{\\rm ph}\\pi R^2f_{\\rm frame}\\,\\Omega_{\\rm FOV}}{\\rho\\,A_{\\rm rx}\\Omega_{\\rm cell}\\,\\eta}\\end{aligned} \\tag{139}$$",
   "why": "Splits the very wide display equation over two lines in one block; the tag is unchanged."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-058",
   "finding": "report #39",
   "old": "Both powers grow as $R^2\\Omega_{\\rm FOV}$, which is why range squared times field solid angle is used as a figure of merit elsewhere in this section.",
   "new": "Both powers grow as $R^2\\Omega_{\\rm FOV}$, which is why §C10.2b and the product comparison below rank by $R^2\\Omega$.",
   "why": "Points to the R^2 Omega definition instead of restating it."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-059",
   "finding": "report #8",
   "old": "1. **In this fixed-power, fixed-threshold example, gap-free single-mode coverage of a full automotive field to 200 m is demanding.** A coherent design must allocate range by elevation: 200 m within a ±1.5° horizon band and 50 m elsewhere cuts the required power about **5.7×**. Required power scales as $R^2$ times solid angle (Eq. (139)), so the mix costs $[3^\\circ\\times1+22^\\circ\\times(50/200)^2]/25^\\circ=0.175$ of the uniform 200 m case, and $1/0.175=5.7$. It must also narrow its field or point rate. That is exactly how FMCW products position themselves: as long-range, narrower-field truck sensors (§C7, §F6).",
   "new": "1. **In this fixed-threshold example, gap-free single-mode coverage of a full automotive field to 200 m needs 20–184 W, even before speckle fading (caveat 4).** A coherent design must allocate range by elevation: 200 m within a ±1.5° horizon band and 50 m elsewhere cuts the required power about **5.7×**. Required power scales as $R^2$ times solid angle (Eq. (139)), so the mix costs $[3^\\circ\\times1+22^\\circ\\times(50/200)^2]/25^\\circ=0.175$ of the uniform 200 m case, and $1/0.175=5.7$. It must also narrow its field, lower its point rate or accept sparse footprints. Some FMCW products are long-range, narrower-field truck sensors (§C7, §F6), while the scanned 120°-wide ones sample sparsely (the $R^2\\Omega$ table below).",
   "why": "Replaces the vague 'is demanding' with the table's figure, and matches the product table, where scanned FMCW units cover 120°."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-060",
   "finding": "report #12; symbol scheme",
   "old": "Because the ratio scales as $\\theta^2$, with $\\theta$ the cell width, parity needs $\\theta=0.1^\\circ/\\sqrt{87}=0.011^\\circ$ to $0.1^\\circ/\\sqrt{800}=0.0035^\\circ$; the diffraction limit itself is at $\\theta=\\lambda/\\sqrt{A_{\\rm rx}}=0.905\\ \\mu{\\rm m}/22\\ {\\rm mm}=4.1\\times10^{-5}$ rad $=0.0023^\\circ$.",
   "new": "Because the ratio scales as $\\theta_{\\rm cell}^2$, with $\\theta_{\\rm cell}$ the cell width, parity needs $\\theta_{\\rm cell}=0.1^\\circ/\\sqrt{87}=0.011^\\circ$ to $0.1^\\circ/\\sqrt{800}=0.0035^\\circ$; the diffraction limit itself is at $\\theta_{\\rm cell}=\\lambda/\\sqrt{A_{\\rm rx}}=0.905\\ \\mu{\\rm m}/22\\ {\\rm mm}=4.1\\times10^{-5}$ rad $=0.0023^\\circ$.",
   "why": "Cell-width symbol from the scheme; theta alone is the beam or emission angle elsewhere in §C10."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-061",
   "finding": "follow-up residual (report #4)",
   "old": "   - Sunlight raises the ToF $N_{\\rm req}$ (§C6(d)).",
   "new": "   - Sunlight is already included in the ToF $N_{\\rm req}$, which corresponds to bright sun; a fully sunlit scene of reflectance 0.9 raises the ideal requirement only to ≈20 (§C0 subsection 10.3; background model in §C6(d)).",
   "why": "The caveat contradicted the Sunlight assumption above, which takes N_req = 20 as the bright-sun value."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-062",
   "finding": "report #16",
   "old": "- **A larger useful aperture can buy photons at the cost of sparser angular sampling.**",
   "new": "- **A larger useful aperture can buy photons at the cost of coverage: the sampling pitch is unchanged, but narrow footprints leave gaps between samples.**",
   "why": "Waymo's example keeps the 0.05° pitch; what it gives up is coverage."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-063",
   "finding": "report #40; report #12; symbol scheme",
   "old": "  - It takes a **$w_0=10$ mm** beam, where $w_0$ is the beam's 1/e² waist radius at the aperture. The derived nominal footprint fraction on the 0.05° grid is about 0.6% with a FWHM-area convention, or about 1.6–1.8% with the Gaussian 1/e²-area convention of §C10.3b [E: the Rayleigh range (the distance over which a Gaussian beam stays roughly collimated) is $z_R=\\pi w_0^2/\\lambda=\\pi(10\\ {\\rm mm})^2/1.55\\ \\mu{\\rm m}=203$ m, so at 250 m the 1/e² radius is $w=w_0\\sqrt{1+(R/z_R)^2}=15.9$ mm and $\\pi w^2/(R\\theta)^2=7.9\\ {\\rm cm^2}/(21.8\\ {\\rm cm})^2=1.66\\%$, where $R\\theta$ is the width of one 0.05° cell at 250 m (1.8% for the 20 mm-aperture model of §C10.3b, Result 2). The FWHM circle encloses $\\ln2/2=0.35$ of the 1/e² area, giving $0.35\\times1.66\\%=0.58\\%$]. They differ by convention and are not inconsistent measurements of scene coverage.",
   "new": "  - It takes a **$w_0=10$ mm** beam, where $w_0$ is the beam's 1/e² waist radius at the aperture. The derived nominal footprint fraction on the 0.05° grid is about 0.6% with a FWHM-area convention, or about 1.6–1.8% with the Gaussian 1/e²-area convention of §C10.3b. The two figures differ only in the contour convention; neither measures scene coverage. *How the numbers are derived* [E]:\n    - The Rayleigh range, the distance over which a Gaussian beam stays roughly collimated, is $z_R=\\pi w_0^2/\\lambda=\\pi(10\\ {\\rm mm})^2/1.55\\ \\mu{\\rm m}=203$ m, so at $R=250$ m the 1/e² radius is $w=w_0\\sqrt{1+(R/z_R)^2}=15.9$ mm.\n    - One 0.05° cell is $R\\theta_{\\rm cell}=250\\ {\\rm m}\\times8.73\\times10^{-4}\\ {\\rm rad}=21.8$ cm wide at 250 m, so the 1/e² fraction is $\\pi w^2/(R\\theta_{\\rm cell})^2=7.9\\ {\\rm cm^2}/(21.8\\ {\\rm cm})^2=1.66\\%$. The 20 mm-aperture model of §C10.3b (Result 2) gives 1.8%.\n    - The FWHM circle encloses $\\ln2/2=0.35$ of the 1/e² area, giving $0.35\\times1.66\\%=0.58\\%$.",
   "why": "Moves a 90-word bracketed derivation into a derivation list and replaces the double negative."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-064",
   "finding": "report #7",
   "old": "  - It needs about 50 photons per detection, a −106 dB link (the ratio of received to transmitted power) and 0.52 µJ per shot at 1.2×10⁷ shots/s.",
   "new": "  - It needs about 50 detected photons for a detection, with four speckles averaged [P]. That is close to the 45 that four looks need in one bin (§C10.2, item 5) and to the 47.3 for one chirp ramp searched in full (§C0 subsection 10.3); requiring the echo's peak in both ramps, as the report's per-point rule does, raises it to 88.5. The link is −106 dB (the ratio of received to transmitted power), and each shot carries 0.52 µJ at $1.2\\times10^{7}$ shots/s.",
   "why": "Reconciles Waymo's 50 photons with the 130 for one fading look in §C10.2, item 5."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-065",
   "finding": "report #21",
   "old": "- **Other coherent solid-state designs show the same choice.** These illustrative footprint fractions are inferred from beam width and address pitch, not field-measured detection probabilities (Berkeley FPSA is a focal-plane switch array):\n\n  | Design | Nominal footprint fraction per frame |\n  |---|---|\n  | Analog Photonics FS-4S | about 3.6% (box convention; 2.8% as a FWHM ellipse; ≈8% at 1/e² if the datasheet widths are FWHM) |\n  | Berkeley FPSA | about 0.7% (box; ≈0.55% FWHM ellipse, ≈1.6% at 1/e²) |\n  | Waymo's worked example | about 0.6% (FWHM-area convention; about 1.6–1.8% at 1/e²) |\n  | A ToF column receiver | its pixels tile the field (about 100%) |",
   "new": "- **Other coherent solid-state designs show the same choice.** These illustrative footprint fractions are inferred from beam width and address pitch, not field-measured detection probabilities (Berkeley FPSA is a focal-plane switch array). The table gives each nominal footprint fraction per frame in three contour conventions: the rectangle of the quoted widths (box), the FWHM ellipse, and the Gaussian 1/e² contour of §C10.3b.\n\n  | Design | Box (quoted widths) | FWHM ellipse | Gaussian 1/e² |\n  |---|---|---|---|\n  | Analog Photonics FS-4S (§C10.2b) | ≈3.6% | ≈2.8% | ≈8%, if the datasheet widths are FWHM |\n  | Berkeley FPSA (§C10.4) | ≈0.7% | ≈0.55% | ≈1.6% |\n  | Waymo's worked example (above) | — | ≈0.6% | ≈1.6–1.8% |\n  | A ToF column receiver | ≈100%: its pixels tile the field | ≈100% | ≈100% |",
   "why": "One column per convention, so rows compare like with like, with pointers to where each value is derived."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-066",
   "finding": "symbol scheme (scan rate); consistency flag (walk-off beam width)",
   "old": "$$\\Delta\\theta\\approx\\frac{2R\\,\\omega}{c} \\tag{141}$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the range, $\\omega$ the angular scan rate of the beam (rad/s; §C0, §C4d and §C7 write it $\\Omega$, but §C10 keeps $\\Omega$ for solid angles), $2R/c$ the round-trip time and $c$ the speed of light. A single-mode receiver loses signal once this walk-off becomes comparable with the beam's own divergence, because the returning light no longer overlaps the receive mode.\n- For a 360° spin at 10 Hz and 250 m, the walk-off is about 0.006°, larger than a 0.003° beam. [E: $\\omega=3{,}600^\\circ/{\\rm s}=62.8$ rad/s, so from Eq. (141) $\\Delta\\theta=2\\times250\\ {\\rm m}\\times62.8\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}/(3\\times10^{8}\\ {\\rm m\\,s^{-1}})=1.05\\times10^{-4}$ rad $=0.006^\\circ$; the beam figure is the far-field 1/e² half-angle of a $w_0=10$ mm beam, $\\lambda/(\\pi w_0)=4.9\\times10^{-5}$ rad $=0.0028^\\circ$.]",
   "new": "$$\\Delta\\theta\\approx\\frac{2R\\,\\dot\\theta}{c} \\tag{141}$$\n\nwhere $R$ is the range, $\\dot\\theta$ the angular scan rate of the beam (rad/s), $2R/c$ the round-trip time and $c$ the speed of light. A single-mode receiver loses signal once this walk-off becomes comparable with the beam's own divergence, because the returning light no longer overlaps the receive mode.\n- For a 360° spin at 10 Hz and 250 m, the walk-off is about 0.006°, larger than the beam's 0.0036° angular radius at that range. [E: $\\dot\\theta=3{,}600^\\circ/{\\rm s}=62.8$ rad/s, so from Eq. (141) $\\Delta\\theta=2\\times250\\ {\\rm m}\\times62.8\\ {\\rm s^{-1}}/(3\\times10^{8}\\ {\\rm m\\,s^{-1}})=1.05\\times10^{-4}$ rad $=0.006^\\circ$. The beam figure is the 1/e² radius of the $w_0=10$ mm beam at 250 m divided by the range, $15.9\\ {\\rm mm}/250\\ {\\rm m}=6.4\\times10^{-5}$ rad $=0.0036^\\circ$, as in §C10.3a; the far-field half-angle $\\lambda/(\\pi w_0)=4.9\\times10^{-5}$ rad $=0.0028^\\circ$ is smaller because 250 m is only $1.2z_R$.]",
   "why": "Uses the scheme's scan-rate symbol (so the note on other sections' symbols goes) and the same 0.0036° beam as §C10.3a for this case."
  },
  {
   "id": "09_C10_part1-067",
   "finding": "report #35, #39",
   "old": "**Reality check: range × field ($R^2\\Omega$) of coherent and ToF products** [P vendor or peer-reviewed figures; E metric]\n\nThis comparison ranks products by $R^2\\Omega$, the specified range $R$ squared times the solid angle $\\Omega$ of the field, because the photon budget for covering a field scales that way (Eq. (139)). Scanned coherent products claim $R^2\\Omega$ comparable to scanned ToF, at higher power; solid-state coherent designs sit one to two orders of magnitude below.",
   "new": "**Reality check: range² × field ($R^2\\Omega$) of coherent and ToF products** [P vendor or peer-reviewed figures; E metric]\n\nThis comparison ranks products by $R^2\\Omega$ (§C10.2b), because the photon budget for covering a field scales that way (Eq. (139)). Scanned coherent products claim $R^2\\Omega$ comparable to scanned ToF, at higher power; solid-state coherent designs sit about 16–24× (FS-4S) to 280× (Pointcloud) below the Hesai ATX.",
   "why": "The metric is range squared; the gap is quantified (38,700/2,440 = 16, 38,700/1,640 = 24, 38,700/140 = 276); the definition is a pointer."
  },
